dagascar. The fat-tailed dwarf lemur is 8–9 in (200–230 mm) long from its head to the end of its torso, with an 8–11 in (200–280 mm) tail extending beyond that.","revisionId":1362993713,"title":"Fat-tailed dwarf lemur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fat-tailed_dwarf_lemur","wikidataId":"Q205837"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"primate","source":"The Paleobiology Database"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Primates","confidence":94,"rank":"order","scientificName":"Primates","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":798},"ottId":913935,"rank":"order","scientificName":"Primates","synonyms":["Ceciliolemuridae"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Primates is an order of mammals, which is further divided into the strepsirrhines, which include lemurs, galagos, and lorisids; and the haplorhines, which include tarsiers and simians (monkeys and apes). Primates arose 74–63 million years ago first from small terrestrial mammals, which adapted for life in tropical forests: many primate characteristics represent adaptations to the challenging environment among tree tops, including large brain sizes, binocular vision, color vision, vocalizations, shoulder girdles allowing a large degree of movement in the upper limbs, and opposable thumbs (in most but not all) that enable better grasping and dexterity.","revisionId":1376327438,"title":"Primate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primate","wikidataId":"Q7380"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Blue-faced Guans","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Sicklewing Guans","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Sickle-winged Guans","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Black and Sickle-winged Guans","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"guan","source":"The Paleobiology Database"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Chamaepetes","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Chamaepetes Wagler, 1832","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2482181},"ottId":963343,"rank":"genus","scientificNam